Subject: x87: Run single-precision PC24 runs natively, with an exact fallback.

Requires 0001. An IR run whose nodes are all f32-exact operations (f32/f64/
integer loads, f32/f64 stores, + - * / sqrt, negation, absolute value and
bridged integer instructions) is emitted twice: a fast body that computes in
single precision, and the existing exact body.

For + - * / sqrt on f32 inputs, one rounding to f32 equals x87 PC24 rounding
as long as the result stays in the f32 range (53 >= 2*24+2). The fast body
therefore checks every arithmetic result, and every value it narrows from
double. On overflow, underflow, NaN, infinity or an input that is not an f32,
it converts its live registers back to double and continues in the exact body
at the same node. The entry checks that the guest uses PC24 with nearest
rounding and that the host FPCR has RMode, FZ, DN and FIZ clear. When the two
bodies would leave different register state at any node, translation emits
only the exact body.

All branches in the fast body and its fallback stubs go forward: Rosetta's
signal state recovery aborts on translated code that branches backward
("unsupported CFG structure"). X87_PC24_RUNS=0 disables the fast bodies.

